{"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"tokens":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"let"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"notations"},{"kind":"text","text":": ["},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNotations","preciseIdentifier":"s:7IvorMXL12MXLNotationsV","text":"MXLNotations"},{"kind":"text","text":"]"}],"platforms":["macOS"],"languages":["swift"]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"kind":"content","content":[{"level":2,"text":"Discussion","type":"heading","anchor":"discussion"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Maps to nested MusicXML ","type":"text"},{"code":"<notations>","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" elements.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]}],"schemaVersion":{"patch":0,"major":0,"minor":3},"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNote\/notations","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"metadata":{"role":"symbol","symbolKind":"property","title":"notations","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"let"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"notations"},{"kind":"text","text":": ["},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:7IvorMXL12MXLNotationsV","text":"MXLNotations"},{"kind":"text","text":"]"}],"externalID":"s:7IvorMXL7MXLNoteV9notationsSayAA12MXLNotationsVGvp","roleHeading":"Instance Property","modules":[{"name":"IvorMXL"}]},"sections":[],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNote"]]},"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlnote\/notations"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"kind":"symbol","abstract":[{"text":"The notations (e.g. articulations, ornaments) associated with this note.","type":"text"}],"references":{"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLNote/notations":{"url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlnote\/notations","role":"symbol","type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNote\/notations","abstract":[{"text":"The notations (e.g. articulations, ornaments) associated with this note.","type":"text"}],"title":"notations","fragments":[{"text":"let","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"notations","kind":"identifier"},{"text":": [","kind":"text"},{"text":"MXLNotations","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:7IvorMXL12MXLNotationsV"},{"text":"]","kind":"text"}],"kind":"symbol"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLNotations":{"url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlnotations","kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNotations","role":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"MXLNotations","kind":"identifier"}],"title":"MXLNotations","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Notations refer to musical notations, not XML notations. Multiple notations are allowed in order to represent"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"multiple editorial levels. The print-object attribute, added in Version 3.0, allows notations to represent","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"details of performance technique, such as fingerings, without having them appear in the score.","type":"text"}],"type":"topic","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"text":"MXLNotations","kind":"identifier"}]},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLNote":{"kind":"symbol","role":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlnote","title":"MXLNote","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MXLNote","kind":"identifier"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"MXLNote","kind":"identifier"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Notes are the most common type of MusicXML data. The MusicXML format distinguishes between elements used for"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"sound information and elements used for notation information (e.g., tie is used for sound, tied for notation)."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Thus grace notes do not have a duration element. Cue notes have a duration element, as do forward elements,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"but no tie elements. Having these two types of information available can make interchange easier, as some","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"programs handle one type of information more readily than the other."}],"type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNote"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL":{"url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl","kind":"symbol","type":"topic","role":"collection","title":"IvorMXL","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A MusicXML parser, normalizer, validator, and formatter."}]}}}